<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter12_text-files-and-stream-states">
    <title>Text Files and Stream States</title>

    <p>
        Text-file programs commonly open a file, perform input or output, test
        whether each operation succeeded, and close the file. File stream objects
        can close themselves when they leave scope, but checking the stream after
        opening is still essential.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_write-text-file">
        <title>Writing a text file</title>
        <program xml:id="program_chapter12_write-text-file"
                 language="cpp" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <fstream>

int main() {
    std::ofstream output_file{"scores.txt"};

    if (!output_file) {
        return 1;
    }

    output_file << "Amina,92\n";
    output_file << "Marco,85\n";
    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>
			Opening an <c>std::ofstream</c> normally creates the file if it does 
			not already exist. If the file already exists, opening it normally 
			erases its previous contents before writing. However, we can avoid 
			overwriting by opening for writing in append mode which we will 
			discus in Section 12.4.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_read-text-file">
        <title>Reading tokens and lines</title>
        <program xml:id="program_chapter12_read-text-file"
                 language="cpp" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::ifstream input_file{"scores.txt"};

    if (!input_file) {
        return 1;
    }

    std::string line;
    while (std::getline(input_file, line)) {
        std::cout << "Read line: " << line << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>
            Formatted extraction such as <c>input_file &gt;&gt; score</c> is useful
            for whitespace-separated values. <c>std::getline()</c> is useful when
            the program must preserve the spaces and delimiters in a complete
            line.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_stream-state-flags">
        <title>Stream-state flags</title>
        <table xml:id="table_chapter12_stream-states">
            <title>Common stream states</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
            <col width="25%"/><col width="75%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>State</cell>
                    <cell>Meaning</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p><c>good()</c></p></cell>
                    <cell><p>No stream-state flags are set.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p><c>eof()</c></p></cell>
                    <cell><p>An input operation reached the end of the stream's input sequence, so <c>eofbit</c> is set.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p><c>fail()</c></p></cell>
                    <cell><p>An input operation failed, commonly because the input had the wrong format or type. <c>fail()</c> is also true when <c>badbit</c> is set.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p><c>bad()</c></p></cell>
                    <cell><p>A serious input/output failure occurred, so <c>badbit</c> is set.</p></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
        <p>
            A read loop should normally use the input operation itself as its
            condition. The body then runs only after a complete requested value
            has been obtained.
        </p>
        <p>
            These functions inspect flags on the stream; they do not inspect the
            file object directly. More than one flag can be set at the same time.
            For example, a failed read at the end of a file may set both
            <c>eofbit</c> and <c>failbit</c>.
        </p>

        <p>
            A stream stores these conditions as state flags. The functions
            <c>good()</c>, <c>eof()</c>, <c>fail()</c>, and <c>bad()</c> query those
            flags. In ordinary reading loops, the extraction operation should be
            the loop condition. The query functions are especially useful when a
            program needs to diagnose why a loop stopped or decide whether
            recovery is possible.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter12_stream-state-flag-usage">
            <title>Stream flags and typical responses</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="75%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell><p>Flag</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Typical response</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p><c>goodbit</c></p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Continue normal processing. No error flags are set.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p><c>eofbit</c></p></cell>
                    <cell><p>The input has reached its natural end.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p><c>failbit</c></p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Check the input format or determine whether the operation can be recovered.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p><c>badbit</c></p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Report a serious input/output problem; recovery may not be possible.</p></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            The member function <c>clear()</c> clears the error flags, but it does
            not repair malformed input or create missing data. A program should
            address the underlying problem before trying the operation again.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_why_not_eof_loop_condition">
        <title>Why <c>eof()</c> is usually not the loop condition</title>
        <p>
            The end-of-file flag is set only after an input operation reaches the
            end of the input sequence. Therefore, checking <c>eof()</c> before
            attempting the next read can allow one extra loop iteration.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter12_eof_loop_warning">
            <title>Avoid using <c>eof()</c> as the primary read-loop condition</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter12_eof_loop_warning"
                     language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::ifstream input_file{"scores.txt"};

    if (!input_file) {
        return 1;
    }

    int score{};
    while (!input_file.eof()) {
        input_file >> score;
        std::cout << score << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            On the final iteration, the loop begins before the program attempts
            the read that reaches the end of the file. The extraction then fails,
            but the body still prints <c>score</c>. If the input contains a
            formatting error instead of the end of the file, the loop may not
            terminate because <c>eofbit</c> is not necessarily set.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter12_extraction_loop_condition">
            <title>Use the extraction operation as the loop condition</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter12_extraction_loop_condition"
                     language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::ifstream input_file{"scores.txt"};

    if (!input_file) {
        return 1;
    }

    int score{};
    while (input_file >> score) {
        std::cout << score << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            This version attempts the extraction first and enters the loop only
            when the operation succeeds.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter12_2_line-input">
        <statement><p>Why is <c>std::getline()</c> useful for a record containing spaces?</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice><statement><p>It converts every line into a binary object.</p></statement><feedback><p><c>getline()</c> reads characters into a string.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>It reads through the line ending instead of stopping at the next whitespace token.</p></statement><feedback><p>This preserves spaces within the line.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It guarantees that the file exists.</p></statement><feedback><p>Opening and checking the file are separate operations.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It automatically validates the record fields.</p></statement><feedback><p>Parsing and validation still belong to the program.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It reads only the first line.</p></statement><feedback><p>A loop can call it repeatedly.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter12_2_read-loop">
        <statement><p>Select all correct reasons to test a file stream.</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>The file may not exist or may be inaccessible.</p></statement><feedback><p>Opening can fail before input begins.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>A read operation may reach end-of-file or encounter malformed input.</p></statement><feedback><p>Each operation can change the stream state.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Testing a stream causes all files to become binary.</p></statement><feedback><p>Stream tests do not change the file format.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>A successful open guarantees every future read succeeds.</p></statement><feedback><p>Later input can still fail.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>eof()</c> should always be checked before attempting a read.</p></statement><feedback><p>The read operation should usually be part of the loop condition.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter12_2_text-code" number="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="show">
        <statement><p>Arrange the blocks to open a text file and print each complete line.</p></statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>std::ifstream input_file{"notes.txt"};</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>if (!input_file) { return 1; }</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>std::string line;</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>while (std::getline(input_file, line)) {</cline></block>
            <block order="5"><cline>    std::cout &lt;&lt; line &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
            <block order="6"><cline>}</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>
</section>
